mode: "100644"
text: "# The FACTS framework — verification as craft, sourcing as strategy\n\nA piece of content is a stack of claims wearing a voice. This framework verifies the stack before it ships —\nproportionately (color gets a glance; load-bearing claims get the full chain). Most published errors trace to\na skipped F (nobody listed the claims) or a skipped T (an AI citation trusted because the link worked).\n\n## F — Find the load-bearing claims\nInventory the draft's claims and triage: **load-bearing** (the piece stands on them — the headline stat, the\ncausal claim, the quote) vs **color** (illustrative, low-stakes). Load-bearing claims get the full FACTS\ntreatment; color gets a sanity check. This proportionality is what makes rigor sustainable at social-media\ncadence — verify everything equally and you'll verify nothing consistently.\n\n## A — Ascend to the primary source\nTrace each load-bearing claim UP the chain: the marketing blog cites the news article cites the report — cite\nthe **report.** Aggregators are leads, not sources. At the primary, check: who ran it, sample and method, who\nfunded it (a vendor study is attributed *as* a vendor study), and what the finding actually says (abstracts\nand headlines routinely overstate). Citing the primary is also strategy: most competitors quote the\naggregator; the account that reads the source becomes the source.\n\n## C — Check freshness + context\nThree decay modes kill true-once claims: **staleness** (a 2015 figure quoted as current), **supersession**\n(later research reversed it) and **retraction** (known-false, however popular). Plus the zombie-stat tell:\ncitations that circle blogs forever without reaching a named study. Context check: does the stat mean what\nthe draft implies (Mehrabian-class misapplication)? Scope check: does the sample generalize to the claim's\nsubject? Date-stamp every stat in the source log so future reuse re-checks age.\n\n## T — Test AI output hard\nAI-supplied citations are **guilty until verified** — the 2026 evidence (60%+ wrong news citations; 12-fold\nfabrication surge; 5-of-45 corporate reports) makes this structural, not paranoid. The protocol per citation:\n**(1) the source exists** — search the title/authors independently; **(2) the link is honest** — a working DOI\ncan resolve to an unrelated paper (identifier hijacking); **(3) the source says what's claimed** — open it and\nfind the claim. Anything failing any step gets replaced or cut. AI is excellent at *drafting* and at\n*summarizing sources you hand it* — the failure mode is asking it to *supply* the sources.\n\n## S — Show your sources\nAttribution that survives social formats: **in-line** naming where the claim appears (\"per the 2026 Lancet\naudit\"), **in-graphic** credit on quote cards and infographics (source + date in the design — route to those\nskills' rules), **links** in caption/comments per platform norms. Keep a **source log** per piece (claim →\nsource → date → link) — it makes corrections fast, reuse safe, and audits painless. Quoting ethics: short\nquotes with attribution, never wholesale lifting, never fabricated or context-stripped quotes (→\n`quote-cards-and-text-graphics`). **YMYL claims** (health/money) carry the heightened bar: official/peer-\nreviewed primaries, qualified language, advice disclaimers. Then the human signs off and **WoopSocial\npublishes** — verification happens before scheduling, never after.\n"
